        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Construct a table to show the conditional probability distribution of antibiotic responses from Bacillus data. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Construct a table to show the conditional probability distribution of bacteria species from susceptible responses. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Construct a contingency table between bacteria species and culture medium.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Conduct a test for independence between bacteria species and culture medium with 95% significance.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Construct a contingency table between bacteria species and antibiotic response.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Conduct a test for independence between bacteria species and antibiotic response with 95% significance.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                <alert> This problem is a portion of your final project </alert> and will involve heavier use of RStudio. Please save all of the code for this problem in an R script named <c>02_LASTNAME_FIRSTNAME_Activity13.r</c>. When you are finished with the problem, save your changes and submit the R script to the corresponding Activity 13 assignment in Canvas. If you are the group leader, you will also submit a copy of the written report for this section to the assignment on Canvas called "Test for Independence Check-in". 
            </p>

            <p>
                <alert>Goal:</alert> In this portion of the final project, you will design a test for independence on two qualitative variables from your data set.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Select the two qualitative variables in your data set that you suspect are or are not dependent. I recommend first considering variables with small event space (few outcomes). Write this hypothesis as a comment at the top of your R script. 
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    You can write comments in R by starting a line with a #.
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Extract the two variables of interest and remove any missing data from them. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the <c>chisq.test</c> command to perform a test for independence between the two variables. 
                </p>
            </statement>

            <hint>
                <p>
                    There are many several different ways you can use the <c>chisq.test</c> command to accomplish this goal. I am not picky about which you use. 
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the results to conduct a formal hypothesis test on the claim you made in part (a) with 90% significance. Clearly state your null and alternative hypotheses in words and symbols. Interpret your results in the context of the problem using plain language. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

</worksheet>
